<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chyhyryn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chyhyryn is a small town on the Tiasmyn river in the Cherkasy region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> it became the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> from the late 1640s. It was fought over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and Russians in the Chyhyryn campaigns of 1677–1678, when it was largely destroyed, and it is now a national historical site.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Ivan Vyhovsky crushed Martyn Pushkar's revolt near Poltava in 1658 with Tatar help and routed a Muscovite army at Konotop in 1659; Petro Doroshenko fought the Commonwealth with Ottoman and Tatar support, and the Ottoman–Tatar army destroyed <b>Chyhyryn</b> in the Russo-Turkish War that followed his fall in 1676.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kholodny Yar is a forested ravine area near <b>Chyhyryn</b>, rich in memories of the Cossack Hetmanate and the 18th-century haidamaka rebels.</blockquote><a href="/state/kholodny-yar-republic">Kholodny Yar Republic</a></li></ul></section>
